    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "I will design a computational experiment to test the hypothesis that minimal network width N_c(w) grows logarithmically with constraint count w for a gradient-flow-governed solution space. The plan: (1) Define a constrained regression problem with w linear equality constraints sampled from a sharp energy landscape (e.g., high-frequency sinusoidal target with narrow basins). (2) Sweep hidden layer width N ∈ [4, 128] for each w ∈ {10, 20, 50, 100, 200}. (3) For each (w, N) triplet, train a small feedforward network (2 hidden layers, ReLU) on w constraint points and evaluate max absolute error on a held-out test set of w constraints. (4) Determine N_c(w) as the smallest N achieving zero violation (max error ≤ 1e-3). (5) Fit log(w) scaling to N_c(w) and compare against linear/exponential alternatives. This directly tests the numeric claim N_c(w) ∼ log(w) using the specified zero-violation criterion."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom ML experiment)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "N_c(w) will show sublinear growth; if the hypothesis holds, log(w) fit will have R² > 0.95 with slope ≈ 1-2 (e.g., N_c(10)≈8, N_c(100)≈16, N_c(200)≈18). If the landscape is too fat, N_c(w) may reach plateau or exceed 128 before zero violation is achieved."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "prediction_inputs"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "plan_id",
      "value": "plan_d83ddf58"
    },
